@rlezuo1 , I take the _samples/drivers/adc_ application as an example, because we see the sequence  adc_channel_setup() then adc_read() that you are describing as faulty. 
For 1st issue, do you mean that **sequence.calibrate = false**, is required in the adc_sequence declaration ?
```
struct adc_sequence sequence = {
	/* individual channels will be added below */
	.channels    = 0,
	.buffer      = sample_buffer,
	/* buffer size in bytes, not number of samples */
	.buffer_size = sizeof(sample_buffer),
	.resolution  = ADC_RESOLUTION,
	.calibrate = false,
};
```

For #2, I see that all STM32 series that have an ADC oversampling (OVSE bit) and an oversampling ratio (OVS bits), the setting  in the ADC CFGR2 "is allowed to write this bit only when ADSTART = 0 (which ensures that no conversion is ongoing). That's why I propose to have 
```
	LL_ADC_Disable(adc);
	while (LL_ADC_IsEnabled(adc) == 1UL) {
	}
```
**before**  `switch (sequence->oversampling)`. 
Then set the LL_ADC_SetOverSamplingScope  and LL_ADC_ConfigOverSamplingRatioShift
Then `LL_ADC_Enable(adc) `afterwards.

- for the same STM32 series, changing  the ADC resolution  "is allowed to write these bits only when ADEN = 0". That's why I also propose to disable(adc) then change the LL_ADC_SetResolution then enable(adc).
